# Master Prompt: Progressive Build — Agentic AI Credit Card Servicing Platform

Paste this whole thing as your **first message** to the coding agent (Claude Code, Cursor, etc.). It sets ground rules so the agent builds incrementally instead of dumping the whole system at once. Then use the **Phase Kickoff Prompts** below one at a time, only moving to the next phase after you've verified the current one works.

---

## 🔧 SYSTEM / CONTEXT PROMPT (send this first)

```
You are helping me build a final-year academic project called:
"An Agentic AI Platform for Single-Interaction Credit Card Resolution,
Real-Time Execution, and Verifiable Auditing"

ARCHITECTURE (do not deviate without asking me first):
1. React chat UI (frontend)
2. Go API Gateway — JWT auth, rate limiting, request routing
3. FastAPI + LangGraph orchestrator — intent classification, slot extraction,
   conversation state management (this is where the LLM lives)
4. Python deterministic Policy Engine — rule-based validation, NO LLM calls here,
   this is the safety-critical guardrail layer
5. MCP (Model Context Protocol) server — the ONLY bridge between the AI
   reasoning layer and the database; exposes a small set of approved "tools"
   (e.g. waive_fee, adjust_credit_limit, replace_card)
6. PostgreSQL — mock core banking database, ACID transactions
7. Elasticsearch — async, immutable audit logging of every interaction
   (user request, AI confidence score, policy decision, DB change)

CRITICAL RULES FOR HOW YOU WORK WITH ME:
- Build this INCREMENTALLY, in the phases I give you one at a time. Do not
  jump ahead or scaffold future phases "while you're at it."
- After each phase, STOP and give me:
  (a) a short summary of what you built and why,
  (b) exact commands to run it locally,
  (c) 2-3 concrete things I should test to verify it works,
  (d) any assumptions you made that I should confirm.
- Do not proceed to the next phase until I explicitly say "continue" or
  "next phase."
- Keep each phase's code runnable and testable in isolation — I want to see
  it working before more is added on top.
- Use Docker/docker-compose from Phase 1 onward so all services run together
  with one command.
- Favor simple, readable code over clever code — I need to explain every line
  of this in a project review.
- Flag any place where you're simplifying vs. what a "real" implementation
  would need (I'll note these as known limitations in my report).
- If a step will take a long time or has a lot of moving parts, break it into
  smaller sub-steps and check in with me between them.

Confirm you understand this working style before we start Phase 1.
```

