## 📋 PHASE KICKOFF PROMPTS (send one at a time, in order)

### Phase 1 — Skeleton & one thin end-to-end slice
```
Phase 1: Build the absolute minimal skeleton, wired end-to-end, for ONE
intent only: "fee waiver."

Scope:
- docker-compose.yml with services: react-ui, go-gateway, fastapi-agent,
  postgres (no Elasticsearch yet, no MCP yet, no real LLM yet)
- Go gateway: single endpoint that accepts a POST with a JWT (use a
  hardcoded test JWT secret for now), forwards to FastAPI
- FastAPI: single endpoint that takes free text, uses simple keyword
  matching (NOT an LLM yet) to detect "fee waiver" intent, and returns a
  hardcoded mock response
- Postgres: one table `accounts` (id, name, balance, fees_waived_this_quarter)
  seeded with 2-3 test rows
- React: bare-bones chat box that POSTs to the gateway and displays the response

Goal of this phase: prove the wiring works, not the intelligence. No LLM
calls yet — that's Phase 2.
```

### Phase 2 — Real LLM intent classification + slot extraction (LangGraph)
```
Phase 2: Replace the keyword matching in FastAPI with a real LangGraph graph
that does intent classification and slot extraction using an LLM.

Scope:
- Add LangGraph state graph with nodes: classify_intent -> extract_slots ->
  route_decision
- Support 3 intents: fee_waiver, credit_limit_increase, card_replacement
- If intent is unclear or slots are missing, route to a "clarify" response
  instead of guessing
- Use [ask me which: OpenAI API / Anthropic API / local Ollama model] for
  the LLM call
- Return structured JSON (intent, slots, confidence score) from this layer —
  do NOT let it touch the database directly

Show me example inputs/outputs for all 3 intents plus one ambiguous input.
```

### Phase 3 — Deterministic Policy Engine
```
Phase 3: Build the Python policy engine as a standalone, LLM-free module
that sits between the orchestrator and execution.

Scope:
- Plain rule functions, e.g.:
  - fee_waiver: max 1 per quarter per account
  - credit_limit_increase: max 20% of current limit per request, account
    must be active 6+ months (mock this field)
  - card_replacement: always allowed if account is active
- Takes the structured output from Phase 2 as input, returns
  APPROVED / REJECTED / NEEDS_ESCALATION with a reason
- Write these as clean, independently unit-testable functions (I want a
  pytest file so I can show test coverage in my report)
- Wire it into the FastAPI flow: after LangGraph produces structured
  output, pass it through this engine before anything else happens
```

### Phase 4 — MCP server + real execution against Postgres
```
Phase 4: Add an MCP server exposing tools for the approved actions, and wire
it so only APPROVED policy decisions can trigger execution.

Scope:
- MCP tools: waive_fee(account_id), adjust_credit_limit(account_id, new_limit),
  replace_card(account_id)
- Each tool executes a real ACID transaction against Postgres
- The FastAPI orchestrator calls these tools ONLY via MCP, never with direct
  SQL — explain to me how this isolation actually works in your implementation
- Add a rejection/handoff path: if policy engine says REJECTED or
  NEEDS_ESCALATION, return a clear message instead of calling any tool

Show me the before/after DB state for a successful fee waiver end-to-end.
```

### Phase 5 — Async audit logging (Elasticsearch)
```
Phase 5: Add Elasticsearch and asynchronous audit logging.

Scope:
- Add elasticsearch service to docker-compose
- Log every request as one immutable document: timestamp, user request text,
  detected intent, extracted slots, AI confidence score, policy decision +
  reason, DB change (if any), success/failure
- Logging must be async / non-blocking — the user should get their response
  before or without waiting on the log write
- Add a simple way for me to view logs (Kibana, or a basic script that
  queries and pretty-prints recent entries)

Show me one full audit trail entry for a completed fee waiver.
```

### Phase 6 — Go API Gateway hardening
```
Phase 6: Harden the Go API Gateway with real JWT auth and rate limiting.

Scope:
- Real JWT issuing/validation flow (login endpoint that issues a token,
  gateway validates it on every request)
- Rate limiting per user (token bucket, in-memory is fine for now — tell me
  if I should upgrade to Redis-backed for the report)
- Proper error responses for expired/invalid tokens and rate-limit hits

Show me example curl commands demonstrating: valid request succeeds,
expired token rejected, rate limit triggers after N requests.
```

### Phase 7 — React UI polish + full conversation flow
```
Phase 7: Polish the React chat UI into something demo-ready.

Scope:
- Proper chat interface (message history, loading states, error states)
- Handle the clarify / rejection / success response types distinctly in the UI
- Simple login screen to get a JWT before chatting
- Nothing fancy — this should look clean in a demo video, not be a design project
```

### Phase 8 — Test pass, docs, and demo script
```
Phase 8: Help me prepare for review/demo.

Scope:
- Write a README with setup instructions and architecture diagram description
- List known limitations / simplifications made vs. a real production system
